538:The basic idea is to let $x$ go to zero fast enough that the bound on $m$ required by Theorem \ref{main} is satisfied when $m = O(n)$, but slowly enough that the length bound is of the form $2^{O\left( n^\lambda \right)}$.
541:Then there is some $\lambda > 0$ so that with high probability, an unsatisfiable formula drawn from $F_k(n,m,c,p)$ does not have a resolution refutation of size $2^{O\left( n^\lambda \right)}$.
547:Then there is some $\lambda > 0$ so that with high probability, an unsatisfiable formula drawn from $F_3(n, m, c, p)$ does not have a resolution refutation of size $2^{O\left(n^\lambda\right)}$.
552:Finally, we can conclude that unsatisfiable random instances from $F_k(n,m,c,p)$ with sufficiently few communities usually take exponential time for CDCL to solve.
554:If $m = O(n)$ and $c = O(n^\alpha)$ for any $\alpha < 1/10$, the runtime of CDCL on an unsatisfiable formula $\varphi$ from $F_k(n,m,c,p)$ is exponential with high probability.
557:If $\varphi$ is unsatisfiable, the runtime of CDCL on $\varphi$ is lower bounded (up to a polynomial factor) by the length of the shortest resolution refutation of $\varphi$ \cite{cdcl-sim}.
560:Therefore with high probability, CDCL will take exponential time to prove $\varphi$ unsatisfiable.
565:By picking a sufficiently high clause-variable ratio, we can ensure $\varphi$ is unsatisfiable with high probability, so that CDCL takes exponential time on average for formulas drawn from $F_k(n,m,c,p)$ (not just the unsatisfiable ones).
568:We also note that our proof technique is not sensitive to the details of how the community attachment model is defined.
571:Let $\widetilde{F}_k(n,m,c,p)$ be the community attachment model modified so that any clause that is not within a single community counts as a bridge clause.
572:Then if $m = O(n)$ and $c = O(n^\alpha)$ for any $\alpha < 1/10$, the runtime of CDCL on an unsatisfiable formula $\varphi$ from $\widetilde{F}_k(n,m,c,p)$ is exponential with high probability.
581:Thus we have showed that similarly to unsatisfiable random $k$-{\SAT} instances, unsatisfiable random \emph{modular} instances (as formalized by the community attachment model) are hard on average for CDCL as long as they do not have too many communities.
585:We have introduced a broad class of ``modularity-like'' graph metrics, the polynomial clique metrics, and showed that no PCM can be a guaranteed indicator of whether a {\SAT} instance is easy (unless $\mathsf{P}=\NP$).
587:While the VIG has received the most attention in recent work on community structure, it would be worthwhile to investigate other graph encodings that preserve more information.
588:Regardless, our result does indicate that it may be difficult to define a tractable class of {\SAT} instances based purely on modularity or its variants.
589:Furthermore, by setting up a concrete barrier (the PCM property) that must be avoided to obtain a tractable class, our result can help guide future attempts to find a graph metric that does work.
591:Our result on the community attachment model $F_k(n,m,c,p)$ is more interesting, as it shows that instances from this model are exponentially hard for CDCL even on average (when $c$ is small enough).
595:On the other hand, when $p < 1$ it is conceivable that the bridge clauses could actually make the instances easier, by adding some extra propagation power or easier-to-find contradictions that would make the whole instance easier to solve than any individual community.
598:The case $p = 1$ also brings out an important caveat when interpreting our result as evidence that community structure doesn't explain CDCL's effectiveness on industrial instances.
599:Our result shows that such structure isn't enough to bring random formulas down from exponential-time-on-average to polynomial-time-on-average.
600:However, it could decrease the time from (say) $2^{n^{1/2}}$ to $2^{n^{1/4}}$, which could be the difference between intractability and tractability if $n$ is small enough.
606:In fact it is easy to see that the result cannot hold in this case: if one of the communities is unsatisfiable then it will have a polynomial-length resolution refutation, and as $c \rightarrow \infty$ the probability that at least one community is unsatisfiable by itself goes to $1$.
607:So with high probability the entire instance has a short refutation, and CDCL could in theory solve it in polynomial time.
